      "tier": "core",
      "verdict": {
        "ticker": "AMD",
        "stance": "watch",
        "previousStance": "watch",
        "view": "His case is Helios plus CPUs plus copying NVIDIA's neocloud fundraise-into-GPUs flywheel, and the quarter supports the direction: record Q2 revenue of $11.5B, up 50%, data center $6.7B, up 107%, now 58% of the company, with Q3 guided to roughly $13B and data-center AI guided to well over 100% growth. The MI400 series shipping makes rack-scale accelerators a two-vendor market, which genuinely helps every supplier in this book. What it does not do is make AMD the better stock. It guides about 56% gross margin against NVIDIA's 74.9% \u2014 it is winning volume at structurally worse economics, so the bet is share, not margin. A $13B quarter leaves very little tolerance for an MI400 ramp slipping by even one quarter.",
        "execution": "No new money at $470. AMD is best held here as a supplier-thesis confirmation rather than a position: it is the reason to own HBM, substrate and optics names, not the cleanest way to own the theme itself. Reconsider if gross margin guides above 58% while data-center growth holds.",
        "changesMind": "Gross margin guided above 58% on a data-center quarter above $8B would show it is winning share without giving away the economics, and would move this to accumulate.",
        "basedOnThesisIds": [
          "r_020df90c96ddf765",
          "r_95e6f060751b9516",
          "r_fd7f012b5ff37e9f",
          "h_46c927b8e8e6582f"
        ],
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-21T09:00:00Z"
      }
