        "category": "memory",
        "narrative": "He is firmly against the view that the memory cycle is over, and this week he argued it from the NAND side rather than DRAM. Phison's CEO expects 2027 capacity constraints more severe than 2026, on the reasoning that upstream suppliers need up to four years from plant construction and equipment investment to actual production, so with demand growing explosively and supply expansion limited the shortage should last years.\n\nSanDisk is his cleanest expression of it. He reads the investor day as removing the cyclicality argument outright: roughly 80% adjusted gross margins projected through 2030, around 75% operating margins and about 50% adjusted free cash flow margins, with long-term agreements already covering two thirds of 2028 output. He cites minimum contracted revenue reaching $93B against a market capitalisation near $239B and asks how a company with revenue targets running four years out can be called cyclical. He notes CXMT signing multi-year DRAM agreements as evidence the whole industry is converging on the same LTA playbook, and that conventional DRAM gross margins at Micron are estimated to reach an unprecedented level by 2027, surpassing HBM margins.\n\nHe is also extending the pattern outward. Watching AI-server MLCC lead times stretch \u2014 Samsung near 40 weeks, Murata moving 24 to 30 to 36 weeks, with new capacity postponed from Q4 2026 into 2027 \u2014 he expects the same crowding-out squeeze to hit consumer and general-purpose grades in late H2 or early 2027, explicitly comparing it to what happened to legacy DRAM.",
