        "category": "robotics",
        "narrative": "The Unitree listing is the event he has been waiting for. Unitree went public and closed far above its IPO valuation, and he reads that as a major public benchmark proving demand for pure-play humanoid exposure is much larger than public markets expected. He has been tracking the oversubscription and the derivatives-implied opening for days beforehand, and frames China as currently leading the hardware race \u2014 citing Unitree claims that their humanoids now run faster than Usain Bolt and jump higher than any human.\n\nAgility is his US expression, held through the Churchill vehicle. He bought between 0.25% and 0.75% of the float around announcement and expects the Unitree listing and other Chinese IPOs to lift it, since Agility has lagged its expected opening valuation. His operating argument is that Agility targets commercialisation first \u2014 Digit inside Amazon and Mercado Libre warehouses \u2014 and that delivery and packaging robots do not need to do backflips, making Boston Dynamics the closer peer for capability and Agility the closer peer for revenue. He explicitly excludes Tesla as the pure-play: Optimus sits inside a company worth over a trillion dollars, and he does not think Tesla can pull off general-purpose robotics yet.\n\nOn components he is more cautious than the theme suggests. Harmonic, LeaderDrive and Schaeffler appear almost everywhere \u2014 Schaeffler claims work with around 45 humanoid players \u2014 and smaller suppliers are niche possibilities, but he expects Chinese suppliers into Agibot and Unitree to book revenue first.",
