        "narrative": "He treats the hyperscalers primarily as demand signals rather than positions, and this week almost every mention pointed at a supplier instead. Amazon's ASIC programme is ramping and he says the Trainium ramp is arriving right about now \u2014 but the position he names is Alchip, not Amazon. Microsoft's Maia 300 capacity is being discussed with an unveil expected soon, and the bullish read he draws goes to Marvell rather than Microsoft.\n\nThe exception is the custom-silicon warrant structure, which he finds genuinely revealing. Google taking options over $12.2B of Marvell across TPU compute, storage controllers, network and memory interfaces \u2014 potentially translating into roughly $120B of revenue through 2033 \u2014 plus Amazon's separate Marvell warrants after the Celestial acquisition, is read as confirmation that the hyperscalers are contracting their way into their silicon suppliers. He connects it back to Jensen's remark that Marvell would be the next trillion-dollar company, arguing Nvidia saw Marvell networking its way into all of them.\n\nThe practical conclusion he keeps returning to is that hyperscaler capex is best owned through the bottleneck, not through the buyer.",
