        "title": "The payer leaves the roster",
        "dek": "Meta drops off the verdict roster on score. The supplier read-through is intact; the equity is still being derated for the spending that creates it.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "Snapshot",
            "body": "$META is a demand node in this map \u2014 one of the four capex payers whose spending funds the [[hyperscalers]] leg of the supply chain."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Thesis",
            "body": "Meta's capital spending is a leading indicator for accelerator, memory and optics demand. Owning the suppliers and owning the payer are, at this point, opposite expressions of the same view."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Why own it",
            "body": "The spend is committed and large: $31.1bn of capital expenditure in the quarter and full-year guidance narrowed upward to $130-145bn. As a demand signal for this book it is unambiguous."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Risks",
            "body": "Free cash flow was $784m in the quarter. The buildout is consuming the cash generation that made Meta a self-funding compounder, and the shares are down roughly a quarter over the past year while the suppliers they fund have multiplied.\n\nThe disclosure also gives capex dollars and nothing else \u2014 no accelerator mix, no useful-life assumption \u2014 so the figure cannot be converted into unit demand for any specific supplier."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Bottom line",
            "body": "No position. The desk already owns the supply side, which is the same view expressed where the cash is arriving rather than leaving."
          }
        ],
